# linear-srgb NEON vs scalar tier isolation — first ARM tier measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench --bench benchmarks -- 'tier_'
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native

## Why this file exists

Before this run, `bench_dispatched_at_tier` could not measure NEON. It handled
only x86 tiers; on aarch64 it printed "Non-x86_64: only scalar tier available"
for v3/v4 and, worse, ran the "scalar" group WITHOUT disabling any token — so
the scalar row was silently re-measuring the NEON path and reporting it under
the scalar name. Adding archmage/testable_dispatch to dev-deps plus a NEON arm
makes both tiers real on ARM.

## Result — tier_neon vs tier_scalar (mean, BATCH_SIZE elements)

| op                                | NEON    | scalar  | speedup |
|-----------------------------------|---------|---------|---------|
| linear_to_srgb_u16_slice_fast     |  2.93us | 19.25us |  6.57x  |
| linear_to_srgb_u16_slice          |  3.59us | 19.17us |  5.34x  |
| linear_to_srgb_u16_rgba_slice_f   |  3.84us | 19.87us |  5.17x  |
| linear_to_srgb_u16_rgba_slice     |  5.34us | 25.84us |  4.84x  |
| linear_to_srgb_slice              |  4.95us | 19.93us |  4.03x  |
| gamma_to_linear_slice_2.2         |  8.70us | 28.93us |  3.33x  |
| linear_to_gamma_slice_2.2         |  8.69us | 28.52us |  3.28x  |
| srgb_u16_to_linear_slice          |  2.93us |  4.94us |  1.69x  |
| srgb_to_linear_slice              |  3.09us |  4.66us |  1.51x  |
| linear_to_srgb_u8_slice           |  2.27us |  2.45us |  1.08x  |
| srgb_u8_to_linear_slice           |  1.23us |  1.27us |  1.03x  |
| linear_to_srgb_u8_rgba_slice      |  3.20us |  3.27us |  1.02x  |
| srgb_u8_to_linear_rgba_slice      |  1.32us |  1.31us |  0.99x  |
| srgb_u16_to_linear_rgba_slice     |  3.35us |  3.28us |  0.98x  |
| unpremultiply_linear_to_srgb      |  4.13us |  3.88us |  0.94x  |  <-- REGRESSION

The transfer-function paths are in good shape (3-6.6x). The u8 paths sit at
~1.0x because they are LUT lookups, which are not SIMD work — expected, not a
defect.

## Open item — unpremultiply_linear_to_srgb NEON regression (root-caused, NOT fixed)

`unpremultiply_linear_to_srgb` is ~6% SLOWER through the NEON tier than with
NEON disabled (4.13us vs 3.88us). It was invisible until this bench could tell
the two tiers apart on ARM.

ROOT CAUSE — src/simd.rs, unpremultiply_linear_to_srgb_rgba_slice_tier.
Per 4-pixel chunk the alpha handling is entirely scalar:

  - 4 scalar extracts        (chunk[3], chunk[7], chunk[11], chunk[15])
  - 4 scalar branches        (if a[i] > UNPREMUL_ALPHA_THRESHOLD)
  - 4 scalar divisions       (1.0 / a[i])
  - then `f32x16::from_array(token, [16 scalar values])`, which stores 16 f32
    to a stack buffer and reloads them as vectors.

On AVX-512 f32x16 is a single register and that scalar prologue is amortised
against wide vector work. On NEON f32x16 is emulated as 4 x f32x4, so the
stack round-trip is 4 vector loads off a just-written buffer (store-forwarding
stall) and the scalar prologue dominates — the kernel ends up slower than not
dispatching to SIMD at all. This is why the regression is ARM-only.

FIX SKETCH (vectorise the alpha path, no stack round-trip):
  let (lo8, hi8) = v.split();  let (p0,p1) = lo8.split();  let (p2,p3) = hi8.split();
  let [r,g,b,a] = f32x4::deinterleave_4ch([p0,p1,p2,p3]);
  let inv = blend(a.simd_gt(splat(THRESHOLD)), splat(1.0)/a, splat(0.0));
  let [q0,q1,q2,q3] = f32x4::interleave_4ch([inv, inv, inv, splat(1.0)]);
  let inv_alpha = f32x16::from_halves(f32x8::from_halves(q0,q1),
                                      f32x8::from_halves(q2,q3));

NOT APPLIED, deliberately. `#[magetypes(...)]` generates one body for every
tier, so this rewrite changes the AVX2 and AVX-512 paths too — and splitting an
f32x16 into four f32x4 forces lane extraction on AVX-512, where it is currently
a single register. That could easily regress x86 more than it gains on ARM.
This host is aarch64 only (Apple M4 Pro); the change must not land until it has
been measured on an AVX2 and an AVX-512 machine.

A cleaner alternative worth considering first: magetypes has deinterleave_4ch /
interleave_4ch for f32x4 and f32x8 but NOT for f32x16 (see
magetypes/src/simd/generic/generated/block_ops_*.rs). Adding native f32x16
4-channel block ops would let this kernel stay one-register-wide on AVX-512
while still vectorising the alpha path on NEON — fixing ARM without touching
x86 codegen shape. That is the recommended route.
